  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        518.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "In the winter of 1636–37, Dutch merchants and brokers met in taverns to trade contracts for tulip bulbs still in the ground, a speculative business they called windhandel, \"trading in the wind\". Prices of prized \"broken\" tulips, with streaked petals, soared."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        410.9614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "In early February 1637, at a sale in Haarlem, the hub of the trade, no one would bid. Days later, at an auction in Alkmaar, one bulb still fetched 5,200 guilders, the Dutch currency of the day, more than a decade's wages for a skilled craftsman. But the frenzy was over. Prices collapsed, many buyers defaulted, and unsettled contracts piled up. Why the market turned so swiftly is still contested."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        303.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        402.4614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Two centuries later, Charles Mackay, a Scottish journalist, recounted the episode in a book on popular delusions, casting it as national hysteria. In his telling, rich and poor gambled, great merchants were reduced almost to begging, and Dutch commerce reeled. His vivid account made tulip mania shorthand for bubbles fuelled by reckless buying."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        156.36141732283468,
        461.8976377952756,
        294.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Yet the archives tell a smaller story. Mackay's story relied largely on satirical pamphlets of 1637 that preached against greed. Anne Goldgar, a historian at King's College London, scoured Dutch records and counted only a few hundred traders, mostly merchants and craftsmen. She found no bankruptcy caused by tulips and no measurable harm to the economy. Many buyers were later allowed to settle their contracts by paying a small fraction of the price, and few were ruined."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        88.46141732283468,
        461.8976377952756,
        147.86141732283468
      ],
      "text": "The boom was real but fleeting. What spread, and still circulates nearly four centuries on, was the moral: a sermon drawn from those pamphlets and retold by Mackay as history."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        537.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1636至1637年的冬天，荷兰的商人和经纪人聚在小酒馆里，买卖尚埋在地下的郁金香球茎的合同。这是一门投机生意，他们称之为windhandel，也就是“风中交易”。名贵的“碎色”郁金香，有着带条纹的花瓣，价格一路飙升/猛涨。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        429.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        529.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1637年2月初，在交易中心哈勒姆的一场交易会上，无人出价。几天后，在阿尔克马尔的一场拍卖会上，一只球茎仍卖出了5,200荷兰盾——当时荷兰的货币——比一名熟练工匠十多年的工钱还多。但是这场狂热已经结束了。价格暴跌，大量买家违约，未结算的合同越积越多。市场为何转向得如此迅速，至今仍存在争议。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        320.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        420.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "两个世纪后，查尔斯·麦凯，一位苏格兰记者，在一本论述大众错觉/群体性妄想的书中讲述了这一事件，将其描绘为一场全国性的歇斯底里。照他的说法，富人和穷人都在赌博，大商人几乎沦落到去乞讨，荷兰商业仿佛天旋地转。他生动的描述，让郁金香狂热成了泡沫的代名词——而这些泡沫由不计后果的购买所助长。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        192.16141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        312.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "然而，档案讲述的故事要小得多。麦凯的故事主要依据了1637年的讽刺小册子，这些小册子宣传反对贪婪。伦敦国王学院的历史学家安妮·戈德加彻底搜遍了荷兰的文献记录，然而只数出了几百名交易者，大多是商人和工匠。她没有找到一桩因郁金香而起的破产，也没有发现荷兰经济受到任何可以衡量的损害。许多买家后来获准只付价格的一小部分，就能结清他们的合同，倾家荡产的人也寥寥无几。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        143.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        183.66141732283472
      ],
      "text": "这场繁荣确有其事，却转瞬即逝。传播开来、并在近四个世纪后依然流传的，是这个寓意：一篇取材于那些小册子的训诫，被麦凯当作历史重新讲了一遍。"
    }
  ],
